0166 | \section{Deterministic Algorithm}
0167 | \label{sec: detalg}
0168 | 
0169 | We prove Theorem~\ref{thm:det}, providing a simple deterministic algorithm. The novel ideas carry over neatly to the randomized algorithm.
0170 | 
0171 | We start by looking at the polynomial $A(x) = (1+x^{a_1})\cdots(1+x^{a_n})$. Note that this polynomial is $\Pi$-$O(n)$-sparse, and that when expanded, each coefficient in the polynomial is bounded from above by $nt$.
0172 | 
0173 | \begin{claim}
0174 | There is a prime $q \in (nt, nt \log nt]$, such that $q-1 = 2^k r$, where $r$ is odd and at most $q^{1/2}$.
0175 | \end{claim}
0176 | 
0177 | \begin{proof}
0178 | 
0179 | Let $2^k$ be the smallest power of two larger than $(nt \log nt)^{1/2}$. We then estimate the number of primes that are 1 ($\bmod \;2^k$) and lie in the range $(nt, nt \log nt]$ using the theorem stated above. We have, for large enough $n$ and $t$:
0180 | \begin{align*}
0181 | \pi(nt \log nt; 2^k, 1) - \pi(nt; 2^k, 1) &= \frac{1}{2^{k-1}}\left[\frac{nt \log nt}{\log (nt \log nt)} - \frac{nt}{\log nt}\right] + E\left(nt\log nt, 2^k, 1\right) - E\left(nt, 2^k, 1\right)\\
0182 | &\geq \frac{1}{2^{k-1}}\left[\frac{nt \log nt}{\log (nt \log nt)} - \frac{2nt}{\log nt}\right]\\
0183 | &= \frac{nt}{2^{k-1}}\left[\frac{\log nt}{\log (nt \log nt)} - \frac{2}{\log nt}\right]\\
